{"id": "2026-11-06-news-knesset-majority-backs-eisenkot-over-netanyahu", "date": "2026-11-06", "stream": "news", "headline": "Knesset majority backs Eisenkot over Netanyahu", "summary": "A majority of Israel's new Knesset, 67 of 120 members, recommended opposition leader Gadi Eisenkot to form the next government, against 46 for Prime Minister Benjamin Netanyahu, and President Isaac Herzog said on Thursday 5 November that he will name his candidate on Sunday 8 November.", "url": "https://www.aljazeera.com/news/2026/11/6/israels-herzog-to-name-a-candidate-to-form-government-on-sunday", "source_domain": "aljazeera.com", "tier": "T2", "tags": [], "topics": ["geopolitics", "politics"], "importance": 3, "display_body": "A majority of Israel's new Knesset, 67 of 120 members, recommended opposition leader Gadi Eisenkot to form the next government, against 46 for Prime Minister Benjamin Netanyahu, and President Isaac Herzog said on Thursday 5 November that he will name his candidate on Sunday 8 November. Herzog announced the date after marathon consultations with the parties elected on Tuesday 27 October. He said that although the \"results of the consultations may appear clear\", \"mutual vetoes between the blocs and within the blocs themselves\" make it hard to say who can actually assemble a coalition, and that he will hold private talks with party leaders over the Israeli weekend. Eisenkot's count includes both Arab-led parties, which won an unprecedented 19 seats, even though he, like Netanyahu, has ruled out governing with them. Netanyahu's Likud is the largest single party with 27 seats, but his wider bloc of allies has only 53, and the ultra-Orthodox party United Torah Judaism declined to recommend anyone. Eisenkot's party, Yashar, says 67 recommendations are the most any candidate has received since 2015.", "why": "for the first time in years a clear majority of the Knesset has backed someone other than Netanyahu, although being nominated does not guarantee a working majority.", "thread_id": "2026-11-06-news-knesset-majority-backs-eisenkot-over-netanyahu", "first_seen_date": "2026-11-06", "event_date": "2026-11-05", "embedding_model": "bge-m3", "emb": "", "entities": ["Israel", "Gadi Eisenkot", "Benjamin Netanyahu", "Isaac Herzog", "Knesset"]}
{"id": "2026-11-06-news-russian-strikes-black-out-seven-ukrainian-region", "date": "2026-11-06", "stream": "news", "headline": "Russian strikes black out seven Ukrainian regions", "summary": "Russian strikes overnight and on the morning of Friday 6 November caused power cuts in seven Ukrainian regions: Vinnytsia, Dnipropetrovsk, Zhytomyr, Zaporizhzhia, Kyiv, Kharkiv and Chernihiv, according to the grid operator Ukrenergo as cited by Le Monde.", "url": "https://www.ukrinform.net/rubric-ato/4182051-bodies-of-53-people-recovered-from-rubble-of-underground-school-in-komyshuvakha.html", "source_domain": "ukrinform.net", "tier": "T2", "tags": [], "topics": ["geopolitics"], "importance": 2, "display_body": "Russian strikes overnight and on the morning of Friday 6 November caused power cuts in seven Ukrainian regions: Vinnytsia, Dnipropetrovsk, Zhytomyr, Zaporizhzhia, Kyiv, Kharkiv and Chernihiv, according to the grid operator Ukrenergo as cited by Le Monde. In the capital, emergency cuts are needed and their schedule may change often. Ukraine's air force said it brought down 196 Russian drones overnight, and energy sites were hit in the Kyiv, Kirovohrad, Mykolaiv and Dnipropetrovsk regions. Separately, the State Emergency Service said on Friday that rescuers have now recovered 53 bodies from the underground school in Komyshuvakha (Zaporizhzhia region) that Russia bombed on Monday 2 November, and that about 50 people are still missing. A presidential adviser, Serhii Leshchenko, said that at the three-way talks with the US and Russia in Abu Dhabi, Kyiv will propose halting the war along the current front line without giving up territory, partial ceasefires covering energy and the Black Sea, and preparations for a meeting of the three countries' leaders.", "why": "Russia keeps hitting the power grid as winter starts, days before talks in which a ceasefire on energy targets is on Ukraine's list.", "thread_id": "2026-11-06-news-russian-strikes-black-out-seven-ukrainian-region", "first_seen_date": "2026-11-06", "event_date": "2026-11-06", "embedding_model": "bge-m3", "emb": "", "entities": ["Ukraine", "Russia", "Ukrenergo", "Komyshuvakha"]}
{"id": "2026-11-06-news-paraguay-s-president-quits-over-bank-collapse", "date": "2026-11-06", "stream": "news", "headline": "Paraguay's president quits over bank collapse", "summary": "Paraguay's President Santiago Peña resigned on Thursday 5 November while the Chamber of Deputies was debating his impeachment over the collapse of the digital bank Ueno, in which he used to hold shares.", "url": "https://www.dw.com/en/paraguay-president-pena-resigns-amid-bank-crisis/a-79868484", "source_domain": "dw.com", "tier": "T2", "tags": [], "topics": ["politics", "economy"], "importance": 2, "display_body": "Paraguay's President Santiago Peña resigned on Thursday 5 November while the Chamber of Deputies was debating his impeachment over the collapse of the digital bank Ueno, in which he used to hold shares. Ueno says it has 2.94 million customers, about 60% of Paraguay's adults. The central bank put it under a resolution procedure on Tuesday 3 November after finding its solvency ratio was below half the legal minimum, and its accounts, which are also used to pay pensions, were frozen. According to DW, the bank held about $800 million in public deposits, and the Social Security Institute (IPS), which manages most workers' pensions, had $355 million there. Thirty-five deputies had accused Peña of conflicts of interest, misuse of pension funds and favouring Ueno; Peña, who sold his Ueno shares in April 2025, resigned after the leader of his Colorado Party, Horacio Cartes, and the president of Congress withdrew their support. In a video on X he said he was resigning for the sake of \"the stability of our country\". Eight senior bank executives, including the founder, have been detained.", "why": "a bank failure that froze savings for most of the country's adults has brought down a sitting president within 48 hours.", "thread_id": "2026-11-06-news-paraguay-s-president-quits-over-bank-collapse", "first_seen_date": "2026-11-06", "event_date": "2026-11-05", "embedding_model": "bge-m3", "emb": "", "entities": ["Paraguay", "Santiago Peña", "Ueno"]}
{"id": "2026-11-06-news-france-marches-over-pay-as-lecornu-calls-talks", "date": "2026-11-06", "stream": "news", "headline": "France marches over pay as Lecornu calls talks", "summary": "Hundreds of thousands of people marched across France on Thursday 5 November over pay, prices and public services, and Prime Minister Sébastien Lecornu called unions and employers to a \"social conference\" at Matignon on Monday 9 November.", "url": "https://www.dw.com/en/france-more-than-600-000-protest-as-anger-grows-over-cost-of-living/a-79860616", "source_domain": "dw.com", "tier": "T2", "tags": [], "topics": ["politics"], "importance": 2, "display_body": "Hundreds of thousands of people marched across France on Thursday 5 November over pay, prices and public services, and Prime Minister Sébastien Lecornu called unions and employers to a \"social conference\" at Matignon on Monday 9 November. The figures differ: DW quotes Interior Minister Laurent Nuñez as saying turnout reached 600,000 by the evening, Le Monde gives the ministry's figure as 575,000, and the CGT union claimed 1.6 million. Five unions from the public and private sectors called the protest, and students, who have been protesting for a month, joined in. Most marches were peaceful, but police used tear gas in Paris and clashed with protesters in Lyon, the Eiffel Tower stayed closed, and the roof of Limoges town hall caught fire. President Emmanuel Macron blamed \"ultra-left\" groups for the violence and promised more police at future protests.", "why": "the conference is the government's main answer to the movement, but a former Macron adviser told Le Monde that \"a social summit means money, and there is no money\".", "thread_id": "2026-11-06-news-france-marches-over-pay-as-lecornu-calls-talks", "first_seen_date": "2026-11-06", "event_date": "2026-11-05", "embedding_model": "bge-m3", "emb": "", "entities": ["France", "Sébastien Lecornu", "CGT"]}
{"id": "2026-11-06-news-najib-s-house-arrest-put-on-hold", "date": "2026-11-06", "stream": "news", "headline": "Najib's house arrest put on hold", "summary": "Malaysia's former prime minister Najib Razak won a court order on Friday 6 November to move from prison to house arrest, but the attorney general's office suspended the move the same day.", "url": "https://www.aljazeera.com/news/2026/11/6/courts-grant-ex-malaysian-pm-najib-house-arrest-after-12m-royal-pardon", "source_domain": "aljazeera.com", "tier": "T2", "tags": ["single-source"], "topics": ["politics"], "importance": 1, "display_body": "Malaysia's former prime minister Najib Razak won a court order on Friday 6 November to move from prison to house arrest, but the attorney general's office suspended the move the same day. It said \"some legal questions regarding the interpretation of Najib's conditional royal pardon need clarifying\". Najib has been in prison since 2022 for corruption linked to the 1MDB state fund. King Sultan Ibrahim agreed in September to let him serve the rest of his sentence at home, and Najib's party, UMNO, reportedly crowdfunded the 50 million ringgit ($12 million) fine, since his own accounts are frozen. Najib denies wrongdoing.", "why": "", "thread_id": "2026-11-06-news-najib-s-house-arrest-put-on-hold", "first_seen_date": "2026-11-06", "event_date": "2026-11-06", "embedding_model": "bge-m3", "emb": "", "entities": ["Malaysia", "Najib Razak", "1MDB"]}
{"id": "2026-11-06-news-top-court-rules-judges-relationship-unlawful", "date": "2026-11-06", "stream": "news", "headline": "Top court rules judges' relationship unlawful", "summary": "The Federal Supreme Court ruled unanimously on Thursday 5 November that federal judges Yves Donzallaz (no party) and Beatrice van de Graaf (SVP/UDC) had been in a \"lasting partnership\" (\"durablement ménage commun\"), which the law on the court forbids for two sitting judges, and it granted a request ", "url": "https://www.rts.ch/info/suisse/2026/article/tribunal-federal-15-affaires-pourraient-etre-rejugees-29407230.html", "source_domain": "rts.ch", "tier": "T2", "tags": [], "topics": ["switzerland"], "importance": 2, "display_body": "The Federal Supreme Court ruled unanimously on Thursday 5 November that federal judges Yves Donzallaz (no party) and Beatrice van de Graaf (SVP/UDC) had been in a \"lasting partnership\" (\"durablement ménage commun\"), which the law on the court forbids for two sitting judges, and it granted a request to reopen a Lucerne homicide case that van de Graaf had helped decide. The five-judge panel decided in a public deliberation, as a pilot case: it found the relationship breached Article 8 of the Federal Supreme Court Act, so the court that decided the case was unlawfully composed. According to RTS, about 15 revision requests linked to the relationship have been filed, including one in the case of Erwin Sperisen, a former Guatemalan police chief. In his plea, Donzallaz called the court's handling of the affair a \"collapse\" and said he was being targeted because of past clashes with the SVP. A parliamentary commission has opened proceedings not to re-elect either judge, and parliament votes on their re-election in December.", "why": "the ruling settles which relationships are banned between federal judges, but it could reopen a series of final judgments, and it comes as parliament decides whether to keep the two judges.", "thread_id": "2026-11-06-news-top-court-rules-judges-relationship-unlawful", "first_seen_date": "2026-11-06", "event_date": "2026-11-05", "embedding_model": "bge-m3", "emb": "", "entities": ["Federal Supreme Court", "Yves Donzallaz", "Beatrice van de Graaf"]}
{"id": "2026-11-06-news-summer-heat-killed-about-1-260-in-switzerland", "date": "2026-11-06", "stream": "news", "headline": "Summer heat killed about 1,260 in Switzerland", "summary": "Heat caused about 1,260 deaths in Switzerland between May and August 2026, according to first estimates the Swiss Tropical and Public Health Institute (Swiss TPH) published on Thursday 5 November for the Federal Office of Public Health.", "url": "https://www.swisstph.ch/en/news/news-detail/news/initial-estimates-indicate-1260-heat-related-deaths-in-switzerland-in-summer-2026", "source_domain": "swisstph.ch", "tier": "T1", "tags": ["new source"], "topics": ["switzerland", "health"], "importance": 2, "display_body": "Heat caused about 1,260 deaths in Switzerland between May and August 2026, according to first estimates the Swiss Tropical and Public Health Institute (Swiss TPH) published on Thursday 5 November for the Federal Office of Public Health. The provisional figure of 1,257 deaths is 5.5% of all deaths in that period, or about 14 per 100,000 people (as RTS calculates it), which puts it in the same range as the 2003 heatwave. September is not yet included. The summer was the hottest since measurements began. About 85% of those who died were 75 or older, and about 70% of the deaths came on \"hot\" days (daily mean of 25°C or more) or \"very hot\" days (27°C or more), the thresholds MeteoSwiss uses for its level-3 and level-4 heat warnings. \"There are signs of adaptation to moderate heat, but less so for extreme heat,\" said Martina Ragettli, who heads climate and health policy at Swiss TPH. Final figures will come in spring 2027.", "why": "heat is again the deadliest natural hazard in Switzerland, and the toll is close to 2003, the benchmark disaster summer.", "thread_id": "2026-11-06-news-summer-heat-killed-about-1-260-in-switzerland", "first_seen_date": "2026-11-06", "event_date": "2026-11-05", "embedding_model": "bge-m3", "emb": "", "entities": ["Swiss TPH", "FOPH", "Switzerland"]}
{"id": "2026-11-06-news-vaud-staff-march-despite-full-pay-indexation", "date": "2026-11-06", "stream": "news", "headline": "Vaud staff march despite full pay indexation", "summary": "Thousands of Vaud public-sector and para-public workers marched in Lausanne on Thursday evening, 5 November, against the canton's new budget cuts, the day after the Council of State granted them full salary indexation of 1.5% from 1 January 2027.", "url": "https://www.vd.ch/actualites/communiques-de-presse-de-letat-de-vaud/detail/communique/le-conseil-detat-accorde-la-pleine-indexation-des-salaires-de-la-fonction-publique-en-2027", "source_domain": "vd.ch", "tier": "T1", "tags": [], "topics": ["switzerland"], "importance": 2, "display_body": "Thousands of Vaud public-sector and para-public workers marched in Lausanne on Thursday evening, 5 November, against the canton's new budget cuts, the day after the Council of State granted them full salary indexation of 1.5% from 1 January 2027. Police put turnout at 6,000 to 8,000, while the organising unions (SSP, FSF, SUD) claimed over 15,000, similar to the first marches of autumn 2025 against the previous savings plan. The government says the indexation follows the October consumer price index, comes on top of keeping annual pay steps, and that it is preserving purchasing power despite tight finances, which it links partly to voters' approval of the \"Baisse d'impôts pour tous\" tax-cut initiative. The unions want every savings measure dropped and the canton's reserves used instead. RTS notes the protest comes less than four months before cantonal elections.", "why": "the Council of State had already dropped its planned \"crisis contribution\" on salaries, and the indexation did not end the conflict over Vaud's 2027 budget.", "thread_id": "2026-11-06-news-vaud-staff-march-despite-full-pay-indexation", "first_seen_date": "2026-11-06", "event_date": "2026-11-05", "embedding_model": "bge-m3", "emb": "", "entities": ["Vaud", "Lausanne", "SSP"]}
{"id": "2026-11-06-news-billing-delays-mask-rising-health-costs", "date": "2026-11-06", "stream": "news", "headline": "Billing delays mask rising health costs", "summary": "Costs in compulsory health insurance fell by 1.2% year on year in the third quarter of 2026, the Federal Office of Public Health said on Friday 6 November, but it warned that the drop is an artefact of billing delays and that real costs are rising.", "url": "https://www.admin.ch/fr/newnsb/Und0pXNIHh9v", "source_domain": "admin.ch", "tier": "T1", "tags": [], "topics": ["switzerland", "health"], "importance": 1, "display_body": "Costs in compulsory health insurance fell by 1.2% year on year in the third quarter of 2026, the Federal Office of Public Health said on Friday 6 November, but it warned that the drop is an artefact of billing delays and that real costs are rising. The delays followed the switch on Thursday 1 January to a new tariff system for outpatient care (TARDOC plus outpatient flat rates). Over 12 months, costs averaged CHF 4,846 per insured person, CHF 60 less than a year earlier, and the drop comes almost entirely from outpatient hospital care (−18%, or −CHF 176). Almost every other kind of care rose, led by home care and nursing (+14.4%), psychotherapy by psychologists (+9.5%) and outpatient visits to doctors' practices (+7.2%). The office says the true trend \"should prove markedly higher\" and will push up the 2027 growth rate.", "why": "a falling cost figure does not mean lower premiums, because the delayed bills will show up in next year's numbers.", "thread_id": "2026-11-06-news-billing-delays-mask-rising-health-costs", "first_seen_date": "2026-11-06", "event_date": "2026-11-06", "embedding_model": "bge-m3", "emb": "", "entities": ["FOPH", "TARDOC"]}
{"id": "2026-11-06-news-italy-pledges-to-resume-dublin-transfers", "date": "2026-11-06", "stream": "news", "headline": "Italy pledges to resume Dublin transfers", "summary": "Italy has told Switzerland in writing that it will again accept asylum seekers returned under the Dublin rules, after blocking the first transfers in almost four years in August, the State Secretariat for Migration (SEM) told SRF.", "url": "https://www.srf.ch/news/schweiz/asylstreit-italien-schweiz-italien-blockierte-asyl-ueberstellungen-doch-gelobt-nun-besserung", "source_domain": "srf.ch", "tier": "T2", "tags": ["single-source"], "topics": ["switzerland"], "importance": 1, "display_body": "Italy has told Switzerland in writing that it will again accept asylum seekers returned under the Dublin rules, after blocking the first transfers in almost four years in August, the State Secretariat for Migration (SEM) told SRF. Italy had accepted responsibility for the people concerned but then refused the actual transfers, in some cases by contacting airlines so they would not carry them, SEM spokesperson Daniel Bach said. Switzerland raised the matter politically in Rome and with the EU. Under the Dublin agreement, Switzerland can return asylum seekers who are shown to have entered through Italy. SEM says Italy's migration authority wrote at the end of October that it would resume transfers, which could happen \"in the coming days or weeks\".", "why": "", "thread_id": "2026-11-06-news-italy-pledges-to-resume-dublin-transfers", "first_seen_date": "2026-11-06", "event_date": null, "embedding_model": "bge-m3", "emb": "", "entities": ["Italy", "SEM", "Dublin agreement"]}
